/**
 * Written verdicts for each curated comparison.
 *
 * The comparison tables are computed from the two areas' data and look
 * the same on every compare page. This is what is specific to the pair:
 * why people genuinely weigh these two against each other, what the real
 * deciding question is, and who should pick which. Keyed by the canonical
 * compare slug. Hand-written; directional claims must agree with the
 * rents and scores in the tables below them.
 */
export const COMPARE_EDITORIAL: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "brixton-vs-clapham": [
    "Brixton and Clapham sit side by side in Lambeth, a single stop apart by bus, and they are two of the most popular places in south London for people in their twenties and thirties. Yet they attract quite different crowds. Clapham is built around its Common and a young-professional social scene of bars, gyms and brunch; Brixton is built around its markets, music venues and Caribbean heritage, and its nightlife is louder, later and more varied.",
    "The transport difference is real. Brixton has the Victoria line from its southern terminus, which means a seat and a fast run to Victoria, Oxford Circus and King's Cross. Clapham relies on the crowded Northern line stations along its High Street, plus the Overground and the mainline trains at Clapham Junction. For a West End commute Brixton often wins; for the City, Clapham's Northern line can be more direct.",
    "Clapham is typically a little more expensive and scores higher for green space and safety, largely because of the Common and its quieter residential streets. Brixton is typically a little cheaper, more diverse and more intense, especially around the station at weekends.",
    "A useful test is to picture an ordinary Saturday. If it involves a run on the Common, brunch with colleagues and a predictable bar crawl, Clapham will feel like home. If it involves the market arcades, a gig at the Academy and a more mixed crowd, Brixton will — ideally on a side street towards Brockwell Park rather than beside the main road, where the noise at weekends is real.",
  ],

  "camden-vs-islington": [
    "Camden and Islington are neighbouring inner-north London areas that often appear on the same shortlist, but they appeal to different temperaments. Camden is louder and scruffier, defined by its markets, music venues and weekend crowds; Islington is more polished, organised around Upper Street's restaurants, theatres and bars, with elegant Georgian squares behind the main road.",
    "Both are well connected. Camden Town is where the Northern line's two northern branches meet, with trains to the West End and the City, while Islington has Angel on the Northern line's Bank branch and Highbury & Islington with the Victoria line and Overground. Islington usually scores slightly higher for connectivity, and its southern edge is walkable to the City.",
    "Islington usually costs a bit more and rates better on safety, while Camden has much better access to green space: Regent's Park, Primrose Hill and Hampstead Heath are all close, whereas Islington has one of the lowest amounts of park per resident in London. Camden's nightlife is more energetic and less predictable.",
    "Put simply, Camden is the better fit for people who treat their neighbourhood as a scene: music, markets, big parks and a certain amount of chaos. Islington suits people who treat it as a service — restaurants and theatres a few minutes away, calm Georgian side streets and a short commute to the City — and who can live without a large park nearby.",
  ],

  "hackney-central-vs-dalston": [
    "Hackney Central and Dalston are about a mile apart, both reached by the Overground rather than the tube, and both at the centre of east London's food and nightlife scene. The difference is intensity. Dalston is the louder of the two, with its Turkish grill restaurants, late bars and small clubs clustered around Kingsland High Street, and it has one of the highest nightlife scores on the site.",
    "Hackney Central is slightly calmer and more varied. Mare Street has the Hackney Empire and the town hall, Broadway Market and London Fields are close, and the railway arches hold bakeries, breweries and bars. It scores better for green space, with London Fields, Hackney Downs and Victoria Park all within reach, while Dalston's nearest large parks are a longer walk away.",
    "Transport is similar in character but different in direction. Hackney Central and Hackney Downs give quick trains to Liverpool Street and across to Stratford and Highbury; Dalston Junction and Dalston Kingsland cover the north–south Overground through Shoreditch and the east–west line towards Camden. Rents are typically similar, with Dalston often slightly higher.",
    "Most people who pick Dalston do so because nightlife is the main reason they are moving east, and they want every bar and grill within a short walk. Hackney Central tends to win over those who still want that energy nearby but also value London Fields, the Saturday market and a quicker train to Liverpool Street, with a little more quiet at two in the morning.",
  ],

  "peckham-vs-east-dulwich": [
    "Peckham and East Dulwich border each other in Southwark, separated by Peckham Rye, yet they feel like different parts of London. Peckham is energetic, creative and diverse, with Rye Lane's shops and markets, rooftop bars and warehouse venues. East Dulwich is quieter and more family-oriented, built around Lordship Lane's independent delis, cafés and pubs.",
    "The data reflects that contrast clearly. Peckham scores much higher for nightlife, while East Dulwich scores much higher for safety and green space, helped by Peckham Rye, Dulwich Park and its calm residential streets. Peckham is usually the cheaper of the two, and the gap widens for two-bed flats, where East Dulwich's family demand pushes prices up.",
    "Transport slightly favours Peckham. Peckham Rye station has the Overground to Shoreditch and Dalston as well as mainline trains to Victoria, London Bridge and Blackfriars. East Dulwich has Southern trains to London Bridge from a station at its northern end, and many residents walk or take a bus to Peckham Rye. Neither has a tube.",
    "The split usually follows life stage. Sharers and people in their twenties gravitate to Peckham for the rooftops, the warehouse venues and the lower rent, and accept a busy, sometimes rough-edged high street. Couples and young families lean towards East Dulwich for quiet evenings, a small-town high street and parks on every side, paying a little more and walking further to the station. Many people do both, in that order.",
  ],

  "battersea-vs-fulham": [
    "Battersea and Fulham face each other across the Thames in south-west London, and both attract young professionals and families with money to spend. The key difference is the type of home. Battersea, as tracked here around Nine Elms and the Power Station, is dominated by new riverside towers with concierge services, gyms and views; Fulham is dominated by long streets of Victorian and Edwardian terraces.",
    "Battersea scores higher for green space, thanks to Battersea Park, and higher for connectivity, with the Northern line extension and rail stations at Battersea Park, Queenstown Road and Clapham Junction. Fulham relies mainly on the District line, which can be slow and crowded, plus buses along Fulham Road and King's Road.",
    "Fulham has more of a neighbourhood social life, with gastropubs, gyms and restaurants along its high streets and around Parsons Green, and a higher nightlife score. Battersea's new district can feel corporate and quiet in the evenings, although Northcote Road and Clapham Junction, further south, are livelier. Fulham is typically slightly cheaper.",
    "Much comes down to the kind of home you picture. Renters who want a concierge, a gym downstairs and a river view, plus a quicker route to the West End, will find more of that in Battersea. Those who picture a terraced street, a local pub where the staff know them and a neighbourhood that has looked the same for a century will be happier in Fulham, as long as they can make peace with the District line.",
  ],

  "shoreditch-vs-bethnal-green": [
    "Shoreditch and Bethnal Green are next-door neighbours in east London, but they sit at very different price points. Shoreditch is one of the most expensive places to rent in east London, while Bethnal Green is usually several hundred pounds a month cheaper for a one-bed, only a short walk or one Central line stop away.",
    "Shoreditch offers the ultimate location for nightlife and connectivity: it scores at the top of the site for both, with Liverpool Street, Old Street and Shoreditch High Street stations and bars, clubs and restaurants on every corner. The trade-offs are constant noise, crowds and very little green space.",
    "Bethnal Green is more residential and mixed, with a daily street market, long-standing communities, large estates and Victorian terraces. It scores better for green space thanks to Victoria Park and its gardens, and its Central line station reaches Liverpool Street in one stop and Oxford Circus in around fifteen minutes. Its nightlife is lively but less concentrated, spread between pubs, local bars and the restaurants along Bethnal Green Road rather than packed onto a few streets.",
    "Think of it as living inside the scene or beside it. Shoreditch makes sense for people who work in the City or tech, go out several nights a week and are prepared to pay heavily for the privilege of walking home. Bethnal Green gives most of the same access, a fast Central line, Victoria Park and a much lower rent, in exchange for a ten-minute walk to the bars.",
  ],

  "bermondsey-vs-borough": [
    "Bermondsey and Borough are both within walking distance of London Bridge and the City, and both appeal to professionals who want to walk to work. Borough is more central and more intense, wrapped around Borough Market, Southwark Cathedral and busy office streets. Bermondsey, further east, is more residential, with warehouse conversions, Bermondsey Street's restaurants and the arches of Maltby Street.",
    "Borough is typically more expensive and scores higher for nightlife, but lower for green space and safety. Bermondsey is usually cheaper and calmer, and while it also lacks large parks within its core, Southwark Park and the Thames Path are close.",
    "Both score equally high for connectivity. Borough has the Northern line's Bank branch and London Bridge's Jubilee line, Thameslink and mainline services within a few minutes' walk. Bermondsey has its own Jubilee line station, which is especially convenient for Canary Wharf and Westminster, and London Bridge is a fifteen- to twenty-minute walk away. Both areas are busy with office workers on weekdays, but Borough's crowds of market visitors make its weekends noticeably more hectic.",
    "If the commute is everything and you want Borough Market and the South Bank on your doorstep, Borough justifies its premium. If you would rather have a Jubilee line station of your own, slightly quieter streets and weekends spent between the arches of Maltby Street and the Beer Mile, Bermondsey delivers that while still leaving London Bridge within walking distance. Both suit people who hate commuting.",
  ],

  "hammersmith-vs-shepherds-bush": [
    "Hammersmith and Shepherd's Bush are neighbouring west London hubs, both in the same borough and both with excellent transport. Each has four tube lines nearby and quick access to central London, and the two are a short walk or bus ride apart.",
    "Their characters differ. Hammersmith has a busy but unlovely centre around the flyover and shopping centre, offset by one of London's loveliest riverside stretches, with rowing clubs and historic pubs along the Thames. Shepherd's Bush has a triangular green, Westfield London, the Shepherd's Bush Empire and the Bush Theatre, and White City's regeneration to the north. Both have a mix of Victorian terraces, mansion blocks and estates, so the character of an individual street matters as much as the neighbourhood name.",
    "On safety and parks Hammersmith has the edge, thanks to the riverside and Ravenscourt Park, while Shepherd's Bush scores slightly higher for nightlife and is typically a little cheaper. Shepherd's Bush's Central line is the fastest route to Oxford Circus and the West End; Hammersmith's Piccadilly line runs straight to Heathrow, and its District line reaches Westminster and the City.",
    "For anyone who flies often, Hammersmith's direct Piccadilly line to Heathrow and its riverside walks are hard to beat, and its residential streets feel calmer. For anyone whose office is in the West End, Shepherd's Bush's Central line is the quicker ride, the rent is usually a little lower, and Westfield and the Empire mean there is always something to do on a wet evening.",
  ],

  "walthamstow-vs-leyton": [
    "Walthamstow and Leyton are neighbouring parts of Waltham Forest, and on the data they look almost identical: similar rents, identical scores for safety, green space, nightlife and connectivity. Choosing between them comes down to things the numbers cannot capture.",
    "Walthamstow is the more established neighbourhood for people moving from inner London. Its long street market, Walthamstow Village conservation area, William Morris Gallery and independent cafés give it a strong identity, and the Victoria line terminus at Walthamstow Central means a seat and a quick journey to King's Cross and the West End. Walthamstow Wetlands and Epping Forest are close.",
    "Leyton is slightly closer to Stratford and the Olympic Park, and its Central line station runs directly to Liverpool Street, Bank and the West End. Francis Road's small cluster of cafés and shops is a growing hub, and Hackney Marshes and the Lee Valley are within walking distance. Leyton is typically slightly cheaper, but its high road is busier and more uneven, and the streets furthest from the station can mean a long walk or a bus before the commute even starts.",
    "Because the numbers barely separate them, the tube line tends to decide it. West End and King's Cross commuters usually prefer Walthamstow, for the Victoria line and the more mature local scene. People working in the City or Stratford often prefer Leyton, for the Central line and the Olympic Park on the doorstep, and they get a slightly lower rent in an area whose identity is still forming.",
  ],

  "ealing-vs-acton": [
    "Ealing and Acton are neighbouring west London areas in the same borough, and both have benefited from the Elizabeth line. The differences lie in atmosphere, green space and price. Ealing is greener, safer and more polished, with large Victorian and Edwardian houses, Walpole Park and Ealing Common. Acton is more mixed and industrial, with a practical high street and a wider range of housing.",
    "Ealing scores higher for safety, green space and connectivity. Ealing Broadway has the Elizabeth, Central and District lines and mainline trains, while Acton's transport is spread across several stations: Acton Main Line on the Elizabeth line, Acton Town on the District and Piccadilly lines, North and East Acton on the Central line and Acton Central on the Overground.",
    "Acton is usually slightly cheaper and can offer better value close to specific stations, especially for renters who work in Shepherd's Bush, Hammersmith or White City. Ealing has a more developed town centre, more restaurants and a stronger family atmosphere, although its evenings are relatively quiet.",
    "Ealing is the suburb choice: leafy streets, big parks, good schools and one excellent interchange at Ealing Broadway. Acton is the value choice, rewarding renters who pick a street close to the right station — Acton Main Line for the Elizabeth line, Acton Town for the Piccadilly, North Acton for the Central — and who care more about getting to Hammersmith or White City quickly than about a charming town centre.",
  ],

  "balham-vs-streatham": [
    "Balham and Streatham are both in Zone 3 in south London, a short bus ride apart, and both attract renters looking for better value than Clapham. Balham is the more polished and better-connected of the two, while Streatham is cheaper, greener and more laid-back.",
    "Balham's big advantage is transport. Its station has both the Northern line and Southern trains to Victoria in around ten minutes, giving it a much higher connectivity score than Streatham, which has no tube and relies on Southern and Thameslink trains from three stations and buses to Brixton. Balham also scores higher for nightlife, with busy bars and restaurants along its high road, and it draws a slightly younger, more sociable crowd than Streatham's longer-settled residents.",
    "Streatham scores higher for green space, with Streatham Common, the Rookery and Tooting Bec Common nearby, and one-bed and two-bed flats there usually cost noticeably less. Its long high street is practical and increasingly lively, and its interwar mansion blocks and Edwardian terraces often offer more space for the money.",
    "It mostly comes down to how much the tube is worth to you. Balham's Northern line and ten-minute train to Victoria justify its higher rents for daily commuters and for anyone who wants a busier social life on the high road. Streatham rewards people who can plan around train times or buses with lower rents, more space in its mansion flats and some of the best commons in south London.",
  ],

  "finchley-vs-hendon": [
    "Finchley and Hendon are neighbouring suburbs in Barnet, both served by the Northern line and both popular with families and people looking for more space in north London. Finchley is on the High Barnet branch and Hendon is on the Edgware branch, so the choice partly depends on which side of north London you travel through.",
    "Finchley rates better for safety and open space, with the Dollis Valley Greenwalk, Victoria Park and its quieter residential streets, and it tends to feel more village-like around Church End and East Finchley. Hendon has Middlesex University, the RAF Museum nearby and Brent Cross on its edge, and its big roads make parts of it feel more car-oriented.",
    "Hendon is typically slightly cheaper, and it has a Thameslink station with direct trains to St Pancras, Farringdon and Blackfriars in addition to Hendon Central on the Northern line. Finchley's Northern line stations provide a direct route through Camden to the City and West End, though peak trains fill up well before they reach Golders Green or Archway, and East Finchley is especially close to Highgate and Hampstead Garden Suburb.",
    "Families who want leafy streets, a village-like centre and quiet evenings tend to settle on Finchley. Hendon appeals more to people watching their budget, students and those who value the Thameslink link to St Pancras and Blackfriars or easy access to Brent Cross, and who do not mind that busy roads shape much of the area.",
  ],

  "barking-vs-ilford": [
    "Barking and Ilford are two of outer east London's main town centres, both offering low rents and fast rail connections into central London. They are close in price, safety and connectivity, and the choice depends mostly on which transport lines and town centre suit you.",
    "Barking has a wider range of lines: the District and Hammersmith & City lines, the Overground and c2c trains to Fenchurch Street in around fifteen minutes, which makes it especially convenient for the City. Ilford has the Elizabeth line, which runs directly to Liverpool Street, Tottenham Court Road, Bond Street and Heathrow, making it better for the West End and west London.",
    "Ilford's town centre is larger and busier, with a big shopping centre and a strong South Asian food scene along Ilford Lane, and Valentines Park is one of the best parks in east London. Barking has a historic abbey site, Barking Park and the new Barking Riverside development beside the Thames. Barking is typically slightly cheaper, and Ilford scores slightly higher for nightlife.",
    "Work out where you commute before anything else. City workers usually do better from Barking, with c2c to Fenchurch Street and the District line as a fallback. Anyone heading to the West End, Paddington or Heathrow is usually better served from Ilford's Elizabeth line station, and gets a larger town centre, Valentines Park and some of the best South Asian food in London in the bargain.",
  ],

  "croydon-vs-sutton": [
    "Croydon and Sutton are neighbouring outer south London town centres, both in Zone 5 and both offering low rents, but they suit very different priorities. Croydon is a big, busy transport hub with fast trains and a large, uneven town centre; Sutton is a quieter, safer suburban town with a long pedestrianised high street and highly regarded schools.",
    "Croydon scores much higher for connectivity. East Croydon has fast trains to London Bridge and Victoria in around fifteen minutes, direct trains to Gatwick and Brighton, and the tram network, while West Croydon has the Overground. Sutton's trains take around thirty to forty minutes to central London and there is no tram or tube.",
    "Sutton is far ahead on safety and parks, with Nonsuch Park, Carshalton's ponds and quiet residential streets, and it is typically slightly cheaper. Croydon has more nightlife, restaurants, venues and new-build flats, but its town centre can feel rough in places and its council tax is among the highest in London. Sutton's own council tax is lower, which narrows the overall cost gap a little.",
    "This is a choice between speed and calm. Daily commuters to London Bridge or Victoria, frequent Gatwick flyers and people who want a lively town centre with new-build flats will get more from Croydon. Families who want quiet streets, strong schools, parks and a settled suburban routine, and who can accept a slower train, will get more from Sutton.",
  ],

  "lewisham-vs-catford": [
    "Lewisham and Catford are neighbouring town centres in south-east London, about a mile apart along the River Ravensbourne. Lewisham is a major transport interchange with new-build towers and a busy shopping centre; Catford is a smaller, cheaper town centre with a strong local character and nearby Edwardian streets.",
    "Lewisham's connectivity is much stronger. It has the DLR to Canary Wharf and Bank and fast Southeastern trains to London Bridge, Cannon Street and Charing Cross. Catford has two rail stations, with Southeastern and Thameslink services, but trains are less frequent and there is no DLR or tube. The South Circular runs straight through Catford's centre, while Lewisham's traffic is concentrated around its rebuilt interchange.",
    "Catford is typically noticeably cheaper and scores slightly higher for green space, with Ladywell Fields, Mountsfield Park and the Corbett Estate's quiet streets nearby. Lewisham has more amenities, a larger town centre, University Hospital Lewisham and slightly more nightlife, although both areas are quiet compared with inner south London. Catford's regeneration plans could change its town centre over the coming years, while Lewisham's rebuilt centre is largely complete.",
    "The extra rent in Lewisham buys frequency and choice: the DLR, several routes to London Bridge and a big town centre, which matters most to people working at Canary Wharf or in the City. Catford rewards those whose commute fits its rail lines with lower rents, more space, parks nearby and a local, less polished town centre that is still finding its direction.",
  ],

  "bermondsey-vs-greenwich": [
    "Bermondsey and Greenwich are both riverside neighbourhoods in south-east London, and both attract professionals who work in the City, London Bridge or Canary Wharf. Bermondsey is closer to central London and more urban; Greenwich is further out, greener and more historic.",
    "Bermondsey scores higher for connectivity, with its Jubilee line station giving direct trains to Canary Wharf, London Bridge, Waterloo and Westminster, and London Bridge within walking distance. Greenwich relies on the DLR to Canary Wharf and Bank, Southeastern trains to London Bridge and river boats, which works well for Canary Wharf and the City but less well for the West End.",
    "Greenwich scores much higher for green space, with Greenwich Park, the riverside and the Old Royal Naval College, and higher for safety. It is typically noticeably cheaper than Bermondsey. Bermondsey has a stronger food and drink scene, with Bermondsey Street, Maltby Street Market and the Beer Mile, and slightly more nightlife. Greenwich fills with tourists at weekends and in summer, while Bermondsey's busiest days are driven by market-goers and brewery crawls.",
    "People who want to walk to London Bridge, use the Jubilee line and have restaurants and bars a few minutes away usually prefer Bermondsey. People who want a royal park, river views, a slower pace and lower rents, and who are happy to commute by DLR, train or river bus, usually prefer Greenwich. Canary Wharf workers are well served by either: the Jubilee line gets there from Bermondsey in a couple of stops, and the DLR or a walk through the foot tunnel does the same from Greenwich. The housing differs too — warehouse lofts and council estates in Bermondsey, Georgian terraces and new riverside blocks in Greenwich — so it is worth viewing both kinds of home before deciding which area suits you.",
  ],

  "wimbledon-vs-putney": [
    "Wimbledon and Putney are two of south-west London's most popular areas for professionals and families, and both offer green space, safety and quick trains to Waterloo. Wimbledon is a larger town with a busy centre and a separate hilltop village; Putney is closer to central London and defined by the Thames and its rowing clubs.",
    "Wimbledon scores slightly higher for safety and green space, thanks to Wimbledon Common, Cannizaro Park and Wimbledon Park, and it is typically slightly cheaper than Putney. Its station is a major interchange, with South Western Railway, the District line, Thameslink and trams to Croydon.",
    "Putney is closer to the centre and has the river on its doorstep, with the Thames Path, riverside pubs and easy access to Putney Heath and Richmond Park. Its station has South Western Railway trains to Waterloo, and East Putney and Putney Bridge are on the District line. Putney scores slightly higher for nightlife, although both are quiet compared with Clapham or Fulham. The Wimbledon tennis championships bring two weeks of crowds each summer; Putney's big day is the Boat Race each spring.",
    "Common or river is the simplest way to frame it. Wimbledon suits households who want a larger town centre, vast open common land, a strong family atmosphere and slightly lower rents. Putney suits those who want to live beside the Thames, be a little closer to central London and have riverside pubs and rowing clubs on their doorstep, at a slightly higher price.",
  ],

  "hampstead-vs-belsize-park": [
    "Hampstead and Belsize Park are neighbouring north London areas on the same Northern line branch, both expensive, safe, green and quiet. They are often compared by people who want to live near Hampstead Heath without moving to the suburbs.",
    "Hampstead is the more picturesque and village-like of the two, with steep lanes, Georgian houses, historic pubs and the Heath at its doorstep. Nowhere on the site rates better for safety or green space, but its hills are steep, its station is deep and served by lifts, and its evenings are very quiet.",
    "Belsize Park is typically slightly cheaper and a little closer to Camden and central London, with Haverstock Hill's cafés and restaurants and the tiny Belsize Village. It is still close to both Hampstead Heath and Primrose Hill, and its white stucco villas and mansion blocks provide a large supply of flats. The Royal Free Hospital at the top of Haverstock Hill is a major employer, which keeps demand from medical staff steady. It scores slightly lower for safety and green space, but slightly higher for nightlife.",
    "Hampstead is worth its premium for people who want the full historic village and the Heath at the end of the road, and do not mind steep streets and very quiet nights. Belsize Park suits those who want most of the same advantages — both parks within walking distance, handsome streets, calm evenings — with slightly lower rents, gentler slopes and a shorter trip into Camden.",
  ],

  "greenwich-vs-blackheath": [
    "Greenwich and Blackheath are neighbours separated by Greenwich Park, and both offer extraordinary access to green space. Greenwich is a historic riverside town full of museums and visitors; Blackheath is a smaller, quieter village beside its open heath.",
    "Greenwich scores higher for connectivity and nightlife. Its DLR stations provide direct trains to Canary Wharf and Bank, its rail station reaches London Bridge in around ten minutes, and river boats run from Greenwich Pier. Blackheath relies on Southeastern rail services from its station, which are quick to London Bridge but less frequent, and has no DLR or tube.",
    "Blackheath scores higher for safety, and both score at the top of the site for green space. Blackheath's village, with its independent shops, restaurants and Georgian architecture, is quieter and more polished than Greenwich's tourist-heavy centre, but it is typically slightly more expensive. Blackheath is also split between Lewisham and Greenwich boroughs, so council services and tax can differ depending on which side of the heath a flat sits.",
    "Greenwich is the livelier historic town: museums, the river, the DLR to Canary Wharf and more going on in the evening. Blackheath is the calmer village: Georgian terraces, the heath, a polished high street and quiet nights, with rail-based commuting and slightly higher rents. People who work at Canary Wharf often find Greenwich's DLR the deciding advantage; people who work from home often choose Blackheath.",
  ],

  "richmond-vs-twickenham": [
    "Richmond and Twickenham are neighbouring towns on opposite sides of the Thames in the same borough, and both offer riverside living, green space and a settled, family-friendly atmosphere. Richmond is the more famous, expensive and connected of the two; Twickenham is quieter and better value.",
    "Richmond scores higher for connectivity, with the District line, the Overground and fast South Western Railway trains to Waterloo. Twickenham has South Western Railway trains only, with slightly longer journeys and no tube. Richmond also has Richmond Park, Kew Gardens nearby and a larger town centre with a theatre and more restaurants.",
    "Twickenham usually costs noticeably less, whether you want one bedroom or two. It has its own riverside charm, with Church Street, Eel Pie Island, Marble Hill Park and York House Gardens, and its homes often offer more space for the money, including Victorian terraces and interwar semis that rarely come up in Richmond at similar rents. Match days at Twickenham Stadium bring huge crowds, busy trains and road closures. Both towns are affected by aircraft approaching Heathrow on some days, depending on the wind.",
    "Richmond's extras — the District line and Overground, Richmond Park, a bustling town centre and one of the most beautiful stretches of the Thames — are worth the premium for many people. Twickenham is the better answer for those who want a quieter, more affordable riverside town with good schools and more space, and who are happy to commute by rail and live with the crowds on rugby match days.",
  ],

  "barnes-vs-chiswick": [
    "Barnes and Chiswick sit on opposite banks of the Thames in west London, linked by Barnes Bridge and Chiswick Bridge, and on the data their typical rents are the same. Both are affluent, safe and family-oriented, but their transport and atmosphere are very different.",
    "Chiswick scores much higher for connectivity. Turnham Green and Chiswick Park are on the District line, Gunnersbury adds the Overground, and Chiswick station, on the Hounslow loop, adds a rail route into Waterloo. Barnes has no tube and depends on its two rail stations and buses, and closures of Hammersmith Bridge to traffic have made it feel even more cut off.",
    "Barnes edges ahead on safety and parkland, with Barnes Common, the London Wetland Centre and its village pond, and it is quieter in the evenings, with a village pond and green at its centre rather than a main road. Chiswick's high road, by contrast, is a busy through route with heavy traffic. Chiswick has more restaurants, cafés and independent shops along Chiswick High Road, a stretch that stays busy most evenings, riverside pubs at Strand-on-the-Green and slightly more nightlife.",
    "With rents this close, it becomes a question of quiet against convenience. Barnes rewards people who want a peaceful village, wildlife, commons and a pond at the centre, and who can accept a rail-only commute and very little nightlife. Chiswick rewards those who want a busier high road, more places to eat, the District line and Overground, and easier access to Hammersmith and central London.",
  ],

  "forest-hill-vs-crystal-palace": [
    "Forest Hill and Crystal Palace are neighbouring hilltop areas in south London, close in price and both strong for green space and safety. They are popular with people who want more space and a creative, community-focused neighbourhood at lower rents than Dulwich or Brixton.",
    "Crystal Palace has the more distinctive identity. Its Triangle of independent shops, cafés, restaurants and bars, Crystal Palace Park with its dinosaur sculptures, and views across London make it a destination in its own right. Rents there tend to be a touch lower, and it edges ahead for parks and evenings out.",
    "Forest Hill is better known for the Horniman Museum and Gardens, Sydenham Hill Wood and its large Victorian villas, and it scores slightly higher for connectivity. Its station has Overground trains through Canada Water and Whitechapel to Dalston and Highbury, and Southern trains to London Bridge in around fifteen minutes. Crystal Palace also has the Overground and Southern services, but journeys are slightly longer and its hills can make station access harder. Both sit on the Overground's Windrush line, so east London and Shoreditch are equally easy from either.",
    "Crystal Palace suits people who want a lively independent high street, a huge park and a neighbourhood with a strong sense of itself. Forest Hill suits those who want a slightly quicker commute, the Horniman's gardens and a quieter residential feel, with Dulwich and the old woodland close by. Both are much better value than Dulwich or Brixton.",
  ],

  "crouch-end-vs-highgate": [
    "Crouch End and Highgate are neighbouring north London villages, both green, safe and family-friendly, but they differ in price, transport and atmosphere. Highgate is the grander and more expensive of the two; Crouch End is livelier and better value.",
    "Highgate scores slightly higher for safety, green space and connectivity. It has a Northern line station on Archway Road, Hampstead Heath, Waterlow Park, Highgate Wood and Queen's Wood nearby, and a historic village of Georgian houses and old pubs. Crouch End has no tube, relying on buses to Finsbury Park, Highgate and Archway and on Great Northern and Overground stations on its edges.",
    "Crouch End generally rents for noticeably less at every size of flat. Its clock tower centre has one of north London's best independent high streets, with bakeries, cafés, restaurants and pubs, and it attracts creative professionals and young families. Highgate's village is smaller and quieter, and its station is a steep walk from the historic centre. Highgate also straddles three boroughs, which can matter for school admissions and council services.",
    "Paying Highgate's premium makes sense for people who want a Northern line station, historic streets and the Heath and ancient woodland on their doorstep. Crouch End is the better fit for those who want lower rents, a busier independent high street and a strong community feel, and who are comfortable with a bus or a walk to the nearest tube.",
  ],

  "bayswater-vs-maida-vale": [
    "Bayswater and Maida Vale are neighbouring parts of Westminster, both close to Paddington and the West End, but they offer very different ways of living centrally. Bayswater is busy, diverse and constantly moving, beside Hyde Park and Queensway. Maida Vale is quiet, residential and orderly, with mansion blocks and the canals of Little Venice.",
    "Bayswater scores higher for green space and nightlife, thanks to Hyde Park and Kensington Gardens on its doorstep and the restaurants of Queensway and Westbourne Grove. It also scores higher for connectivity, with the Central, Circle and District lines nearby and Paddington's Elizabeth line and Heathrow Express a short walk away.",
    "Maida Vale scores much higher for safety and is typically slightly cheaper. Its Bakerloo line stations provide direct trains to Paddington, Oxford Circus and Waterloo, but there is no fast direct route to the City. Its wide avenues, communal gardens and canal walks make it one of the calmest places to live in inner London, but its evenings are very quiet. Bayswater's hotels and short lets mean a faster-changing population, while Maida Vale's mansion blocks tend to hold tenants for years.",
    "Bayswater suits people who want Hyde Park, an international, always-busy neighbourhood and some of the best transport in west London, and who will accept small flats and constant bustle. Maida Vale suits people who want peaceful streets, mansion flats with shared gardens and canal walks close to central London, and who do not need nightlife or a fast route to the City.",
  ],

  // Restored September 2026: pairs that were already earning search clicks
  // before the cluster was cut to 24, now with a written verdict each.
  "wimbledon-vs-richmond": [
    "Wimbledon and Richmond are the two big leafy towns of south-west London, and on paper they are almost twins: the same typical one-bed rent, top marks for green space, high safety scores and a fast mainline run into Waterloo. People shortlist both because they want London without feeling hemmed in by it, and the choice usually comes down to which kind of greenery and which kind of town centre they prefer.",
    "Wimbledon's open space is the Common, a huge sweep of heath and woodland that joins Putney Heath, and its town is split in two. Down the hill is a busy centre of shops and chain restaurants around the station; up the hill is Wimbledon Village, with boutiques, pubs and horse riders on the Common. Richmond's green space is Richmond Park, with its deer and its view from the hill, plus the Thames towpath, Richmond Green and Kew Gardens next door. Its town centre is older and prettier, and the riverside fills up on sunny weekends.",
    "Transport is closer than it looks. Wimbledon's station combines fast mainline trains, the District line, Thameslink and the tram to Croydon, which gives it more ways out in more directions. Richmond has the District line, the Overground across north London and its own fast trains to Waterloo. Richmond sits under Heathrow's approach paths on some days; Wimbledon gets the tennis crowds for a fortnight each summer.",
    "Richmond tends to win for people who want the river, a historic town and the biggest park in London at the end of the road. Wimbledon tends to win for people who want slightly more practical transport, a bigger shopping centre and the Common's wilder feel, with the village on hand for evenings out.",
  ],

  "barnes-vs-richmond": [
    "Barnes and Richmond share a borough, a stretch of the Thames and a reputation for being among the most comfortable places to live in London. They are close enough that people often view flats in both on the same Saturday. Yet the two feel very different day to day: Barnes is a small, self-contained village; Richmond is a proper town with a high street, a theatre and a busy station.",
    "The biggest difference is how you get anywhere. Barnes has no tube, only two rail stations with trains towards Waterloo and a handful of bus routes, so it scores low for connectivity. Richmond has the District line, the Overground and fast trains, and scores far higher. Barnes is also the pricier of the two on our figures, by about a hundred pounds a month for a one-bed, which surprises people who assume the bigger name costs more.",
    "What Barnes offers for that is quiet. Its centre is a pond, a green and a row of independent shops, with Barnes Common and the London Wetland Centre's reedbeds and bird hides nearby, and its nightlife barely registers. Richmond has more going on after dark, with riverside pubs and restaurants, and more green space still, with Richmond Park and Kew close by. Both score at the top of the range for safety.",
    "Barnes suits people who want to feel they have left London, and whose commute works by train or bicycle. Richmond suits people who want most of that calm with far better transport, a lively town centre and, on our data, a slightly lower rent.",
  ],

  "chelsea-vs-mayfair": [
    "Chelsea and Mayfair are two of the most expensive postcodes in the country, and people comparing them have usually decided that price is not the first question. Mayfair is the dearer of the two on our figures, by around two hundred pounds a month for a one-bed and more for a two-bed, but both sit near the very top of the site's rent table, and both score at the top for safety.",
    "The character is what separates them. Chelsea is residential: garden squares, stucco terraces, mansion flats and mews houses between the King's Road and the river, with the Embankment, Battersea Park across the water and a neighbourhood that has school runs and local pubs as well as boutiques. Mayfair is central London proper. Its Georgian streets are shared with hedge funds, galleries, hotels, embassies and luxury shops, and it can feel empty of everyday life at weekends, when the offices are shut.",
    "Transport strongly favours Mayfair. Green Park station has the Jubilee, Piccadilly and Victoria lines, and Bond Street's Elizabeth line is a short walk, so almost everywhere is quick; the West End is on foot. Chelsea relies on Sloane Square's Circle and District lines and a lot of buses, and scores surprisingly low for connectivity for somewhere so expensive.",
    "Mayfair makes sense for someone who works in the West End, eats out most nights and wants to walk everywhere. Chelsea makes sense for someone who wants the same polish in a place that feels like a neighbourhood, with the river close by, and who can live with a slower route into town.",
  ],

  "kensington-vs-mayfair": [
    "Kensington and Mayfair both mean prime London, and both score near the top of the site for safety, walkability and connectivity. On our figures Mayfair costs a couple of hundred pounds a month more for a one-bed, and the gap widens to around four hundred for a two-bed, where Kensington's larger mansion flats bring the price per room down.",
    "The two have very different daily rhythms. Kensington is a place people live: Kensington High Street's shops, the garden squares, Holland Park and Kensington Gardens on either side, and the museums of South Kensington a walk away. It scores much higher for green space and much lower for nightlife, and the evenings are restrained. Mayfair is a business and luxury district with homes in it. Its restaurants, bars, private members' clubs and hotels give it far more to do after dark, but supermarkets, schools and ordinary local life are harder to find.",
    "Both are well connected, in different directions. High Street Kensington is on the Circle and District lines, which are reliable but not fast, while Green Park puts three deep tube lines under Mayfair and makes most of central London a few stops away. From Mayfair, much of the West End is a walk.",
    "Kensington is the stronger answer for families and for anyone who wants parks, space and quiet at a slightly lower rent. Mayfair is the answer for people who want central London on foot, a more social evening and an address that does not need explaining, and who accept paying the highest rents on the map for it.",
  ],

  "putney-vs-earlsfield": [
    "Putney and Earlsfield are both in Wandsworth, a couple of miles apart, and both attract young professionals who work in the West End or Westminster and want a calmer base than Clapham. The first thing to know is the price gap: Earlsfield is around two hundred and fifty pounds a month cheaper for a one-bed on our figures, and a bit more than that for a two-bed.",
    "Putney's advantages are the river and space. Its embankment, rowing clubs and waterside pubs give it a focus Earlsfield lacks, Putney Heath and Wimbledon Common are to the south, and Richmond Park is a short ride away, so it scores higher for green space. It also has the District line at East Putney as well as its rail station, which gives it slightly better connectivity.",
    "Earlsfield is smaller and more local. It grew up around its station on Garratt Lane, with Victorian terraces spreading up the hill towards Wandsworth Common and along the River Wandle, and it has a surprisingly strong run of cafés, restaurants and pubs for its size. On our data it has a higher density of young professionals than Putney and scores better for food and cafés, but it has no tube and quiet evenings. Its trains reach Waterloo quickly via Clapham Junction, which is the commute most residents rely on.",
    "If the Waterloo line suits your commute and you want the lower rent, Earlsfield is hard to beat in this corner of London. If you want the Thames on your doorstep, a tube option and a bit more going on, Putney earns its premium.",
  ],

  "camden-vs-notting-hill": [
    "Camden and Notting Hill are two of London's most famous neighbourhoods, known worldwide for their markets, and both score high for food, cafés and nightlife. But they occupy different ends of the city's personality. Camden is loud, scruffy and musical; Notting Hill is pastel terraces, Portobello Road and a polished, wealthy crowd.",
    "On our figures a one-bed in Notting Hill runs roughly £300 a month above Camden, and scores much higher for safety. Its streets are calmer, its cafés are among the best rated on the site and the Central, Circle and District lines at Notting Hill Gate give slightly better connectivity. Holland Park and Kensington Gardens are close. The trade-offs are small flats for the money and tourist crowds, especially around the market on Saturdays and during Carnival in August.",
    "Camden has more nightlife and more edge. Its venues, pubs and late bars make it one of the most lively places on the site, and the crowds at Camden Market are heavy every weekend. Regent's Park, Primrose Hill and the canal give it equal green space. Its Northern line stations reach the West End and the City directly, and some corners feel rough after dark, which shows in a lower safety score.",
    "Camden fits people who want gigs, energy and a slightly lower rent, and who do not mind noise. Notting Hill fits those who want an attractive, safer and quieter-feeling neighbourhood with excellent food and a Central line commute, and who can pay for the postcode.",
  ],

  "peckham-vs-camberwell": [
    "Peckham and Camberwell are next-door neighbours in Southwark, both creative, both without a tube and both among the more affordable parts of inner south London. Rents are almost identical on our figures, with Camberwell about fifty pounds a month cheaper, so the decision is about atmosphere and how each area connects to the rest of London.",
    "Peckham is the livelier of the two. Rye Lane, the rooftop bars, the warehouse venues and a very strong food scene push its nightlife score close to the maximum. It also has slightly better transport: its station runs Overground services north through Shoreditch as well as mainline trains to Victoria and London Bridge. The trade-off is a lower safety score and a high street that can feel hectic.",
    "Camberwell is a little quieter and a little more grown-up. It has an art school, one of London's biggest teaching hospitals at Denmark Hill, a green at its centre, good pubs and a food scene that is almost as strong as its neighbour's. Denmark Hill station has the Overground and Thameslink, but much of Camberwell depends on buses into Elephant & Castle, Vauxhall and the West End, and journey times depend on the traffic. It scores slightly better for safety and slightly worse for connectivity.",
    "Many people who move to this corner of south London try one and then the other. Peckham suits people who want to be in the middle of things and value the Overground. Camberwell suits people who want a calmer base, work at the hospital or in central London by bus, and are happy to walk to Peckham for a night out.",
  ],

  "camden-vs-kentish-town": [
    "Kentish Town is often described as Camden without the crowds, and the comparison is a fair starting point. The two sit on the same Northern line branch in the same borough, a single stop apart, and many people who fancy Camden end up renting in Kentish Town instead. Renting in Kentish Town saves roughly £150 a month on a one-bed by our numbers, and about £300 on a two-bed.",
    "Camden is the bigger and louder of the two. Its markets, music venues and bars put it near the top of our nightlife ranking, and the weekend crowds are huge. Regent's Park and Primrose Hill are within walking distance, which gives it a slightly higher green-space score. Kentish Town is calmer and scores better for safety. It has its own music venue, the Forum, and a solid run of pubs and restaurants, but its high street is practical rather than pretty.",
    "The two score level for connectivity, but Kentish Town has an extra option. As well as the Northern line, its station has Thameslink trains to St Pancras, Farringdon, Blackfriars and London Bridge, which is very useful for City commuters. Hampstead Heath and Parliament Hill are a walk uphill, and Tufnell Park and Gospel Oak are close by.",
    "Choose Camden if you want the scene outside your front door and do not mind paying extra and putting up with tourists. Choose Kentish Town if you want to be close enough to Camden to enjoy it and far enough away to sleep, with lower rent, a Thameslink station and the Heath nearby.",
  ],

  "west-hampstead-vs-belsize-park": [
    "West Hampstead and Belsize Park are both in the borough of Camden, both on the edge of Hampstead and both popular with professionals, but they appeal to very different people. West Hampstead is one of the most youthful and social areas in north London; Belsize Park is quiet, moneyed and residential, and it is a little dearer, by roughly £100 a month for one bedroom.",
    "West Hampstead's big advantage is transport. Three stations sit within a couple of minutes' walk of each other on West End Lane: the Jubilee line, the Overground and Thameslink, which together give it one of the highest connectivity scores on the site. Its high street is full of cafés, bars and restaurants, and it has the highest young-professional density in this part of London. The trade-offs are traffic on West End Lane and less green space than its neighbours.",
    "Belsize Park is calmer. Its white stucco villas and mansion blocks sit between Primrose Hill and Hampstead Heath, which gives it a far higher green-space score, and it scores better for safety. It has a Northern line station and a small village of cafés and restaurants, but little nightlife, and it can feel sleepy for people in their twenties.",
    "West Hampstead is the natural choice for young professionals who want to go out, have friends nearby and reach the City, Canary Wharf or the West End quickly. Belsize Park suits people who want a peaceful, elegant base with the Heath on their doorstep, and who value quiet evenings over a busy social life.",
  ],

  "hackney-central-vs-brixton": [
    "Hackney Central and Brixton are the east and south London versions of the same idea: diverse, creative, food-obsessed neighbourhoods with busy nightlife and rents that climbed quickly through the 2020s. On our figures they cost exactly the same for a one-bed and a two-bed, and they score the same for safety and green space, so the choice is mostly about which side of the river and which kind of transport you want.",
    "Transport is the clearest difference. Brixton has a Victoria line station, which gives fast, frequent trains to Victoria, Oxford Circus and King's Cross, plus rail services. Hackney Central relies on the Overground from Hackney Central and Hackney Downs, which run fast to Liverpool Street and orbit round to Highbury and Stratford, but there is no tube. That gives Brixton a slightly higher connectivity score.",
    "Brixton edges ahead on nightlife and food, with its market arcades, music venues and Caribbean restaurants, and nights there run long and loud. Hackney Central has more of a young-professional crowd, with London Fields, Broadway Market, breweries and railway-arch bars close by, and it is slightly less intense after dark.",
    "For West End workers, Brixton's Victoria line is a strong reason to pick south London. For people working in the City, Shoreditch or Stratford, or who want London Fields and the east London food and design scene around them, Hackney Central is the better base. Both suit people who want their neighbourhood to be full of life.",
  ],

  "hackney-central-vs-shoreditch": [
    "People who search for Hackney versus Shoreditch are usually deciding whether to live in the middle of east London's nightlife or a couple of Overground stops from it. Shoreditch spans the Hackney and Tower Hamlets border at the southern tip of the borough; Hackney Central is its main town centre, around Mare Street, about two miles north.",
    "The price difference is large. Our figures put Shoreditch roughly £600 a month higher for a one-bed, and £800 higher for a two-bed. For that, it offers the top score on the site for nightlife, food and connectivity. Old Street, Shoreditch High Street and Liverpool Street give it the Northern, Overground, Central and Elizabeth lines and mainline trains, and the City is a walk away.",
    "Hackney Central has most of the same energy at a lower intensity. Its cafés, breweries, restaurants and bars under the railway arches, the Hackney Empire, Broadway Market and London Fields give it a strong local scene, and it scores twice as high for green space. It has no tube, but its Overground stations reach Liverpool Street and Shoreditch in a few minutes. Safety scores are the same for both.",
    "Shoreditch is best for people who go out several nights a week, want to walk to work in the City or Tech City and are happy to pay a high price for noise and small flats. Hackney Central is best for those who want to be close to all that, with more parks, more space and hundreds of pounds a month left over.",
  ],

  "peckham-vs-lewisham": [
    "Peckham and Lewisham are about two miles apart in south-east London and come up together for people looking for a cheaper base with a reasonable commute. Lewisham is the cheaper of the two, saving roughly £100 monthly on one bedroom and £150 on two, and it is far better connected; Peckham has much more going on.",
    "Lewisham is a transport hub. The DLR runs from there to Canary Wharf and Bank, and frequent Southeastern services head into London Bridge and on to the City and Charing Cross, giving it one of the higher connectivity scores in south London. Its town centre is built around a large shopping centre and the station interchange, with new towers and a daily market, and Hilly Fields and Blackheath are nearby.",
    "Peckham has the nightlife, food and creative scene: rooftop bars, warehouse venues and the shops and restaurants of Rye Lane. It scores much higher for nightlife and food and for how lively it feels. Peckham Rye station has the Overground and trains to Victoria and London Bridge, but there is no tube or DLR, so its connectivity score is lower. It also scores slightly lower for safety.",
    "Canary Wharf workers and anyone who puts the commute first will usually find Lewisham the better deal. People who want their weekends to happen within walking distance, and who are comfortable with a rail and Overground commute, will usually prefer Peckham, even at a slightly higher rent.",
  ],

  "highgate-vs-muswell-hill": [
    "Highgate and Muswell Hill are two hilltop villages in Haringey, separated by the ancient woodland of Highgate Wood and Queen's Wood, and each earns a nine out of ten for green space on our measure. Both suit people who want a calm, leafy part of north London, but there is a trade-off between price and transport.",
    "Highgate is the dearer village, by roughly £250 per month for one bedroom on our numbers, and it has a Northern line station, which puts the West End and the City within direct reach. Its old village, Georgian houses, historic pubs and Highgate Cemetery give it a distinctive character, and Hampstead Heath and Waterlow Park are close. It scores slightly higher for safety, cafés and nightlife, although neither area is known for going out.",
    "Muswell Hill has no tube. Most commutes start with a bus down to Highgate or East Finchley or a train from Alexandra Palace or Hornsey, which is why it has one of the lower connectivity scores in the area. What it offers is space and value: a large Edwardian Broadway of shops, cafés and restaurants, family houses and mansion flats, and Alexandra Palace and its park on the edge of the neighbourhood.",
    "Highgate is worth the premium for people who need a tube station within walking distance and want historic streets and the Heath. Muswell Hill suits families and people who work from home or travel less often, and who would rather put the difference in rent towards more space.",
  ],

  "elephant-castle-vs-vauxhall": [
    "Elephant & Castle and Vauxhall are both inner south London transport hubs that have been rebuilt with new towers over the past fifteen years, and both are popular with young professionals who want to live close to central London without paying central London rents. Vauxhall is pricier: our figures show about £150 extra monthly for one bedroom and £300 for two.",
    "Both are very well connected. Elephant & Castle has the Northern and Bakerloo lines and Thameslink, which gives direct trains to the City, Waterloo, the West End and beyond. Vauxhall has the Victoria line, South Western Railway trains into Waterloo and a big bus station, and it scores the maximum for connectivity. Neither is particularly pleasant to walk around at street level, with big roads and junctions at the centre of each.",
    "The atmosphere differs. Elephant & Castle has one of London's best Latin American food scenes and a busy, mixed, still-changing town centre, and it scores higher for food. Vauxhall sits on the river beside Nine Elms, with Vauxhall Pleasure Gardens, a well-known LGBTQ+ nightlife scene and a slightly higher nightlife score, but it can feel anonymous around the new blocks. Green space and safety scores are the same.",
    "Elephant & Castle is the better value option for anyone whose job is in the Square Mile or around Waterloo and want a lively local food scene. Vauxhall suits people who want the Victoria line, riverside new-build flats with gyms and concierges, and a quick trip to Victoria or Oxford Circus.",
  ],
};
